{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789825205703,"sessionID":"ses_f462b3faeffehKvke01c5BeJ5s","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e545650015HxqlY4iaKbXzD","sessionID":"ses_f462b3faeffehKvke01c5BeJ5s","messageID":"msg_0b9e53d50001c82BZH6W4UVix6","type":"tool","id":"call_00_ET_mEwT3LGzYzTlhoiO8U6w2811","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/tmp/opencode/ledger_dump.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/ledger_dump.txt, lines 1-271\n1: ===== R001 | Adviser engaged | round 0 | 10/23/2012 | BofA Merrill Lynch =====\n2: TERMS: Engaged by Mac-Gray as financial advisor for its consideration of a possible acquisition of CSC (the 2011-2012 Discussions - an attempted purchase by Mac-Gray, not its own sale). Mandate later terminated (Adviser ended row); the same bank is re-engaged for this sale process.\n3: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n4: WHY: Stated: “Mac-Gray engaged BofA Merrill Lynch on October 23, 2012.” Context only, per the instruction that a target's attempt to buy another company is not its own sale process; the engagement explains the adviser's later re-engagement for this sale process (R006).\n5: SOURCE: P-003 | PAGE: 27 | REVIEW: Q8 | RELATED: terminated by R005; re-engaged in R006\n6: \n7: ===== R002 | Target sale decision | round 0 | 04/05/2013 | Mac-Gray Board of Directors =====\n8: TERMS: Board authorized management to work with BofA Merrill Lynch to explore transactional opportunities, including opportunities with Party A; discussed the sale of CSC to Pamplona and industry M&A activity; to revisit strategic alternatives on 05/09/2013. An exploration authorization, not the sale-process launch (see R009).\n9: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n10: WHY: Stated: “authorized Mac-Gray management to work with BofA Merrill Lynch to explore transactional opportunities that might be available to Mac-Gray, including opportunities with another strategic party, whom we refer to as Party A, in the laundry facilities management industry” Qualification: the Board only “determined to further consider Mac-Gray's strategic alternatives at its meeting scheduled for May 9, 2013.” Counting: no tally contribution (process-wide decision row).\n11: SOURCE: P-004 | PAGE: 27 | REVIEW: Q1 | RELATED: followed by R003 contact and R009 sale-process opening\n12: \n13: ===== R003 | Contact | round 0 | 04/08/2013 | Party A =====\n14: TERMS: Direction: target-initiated (BofA Merrill Lynch telephoned, as instructed by the Board). First sale-related contact; Party A indicated it might consider a business combination with Mac-Gray. Pre-process (round 0).\n15: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: 1\n16: WHY: Stated: “representatives of BofA Merrill Lynch, as instructed by the Board, telephoned a representative of Party A to discuss generally a possible business combination between Party A and Mac-Gray.” Count 1 = the newly represented bidder unit for the contact step; the same bidder's 06/21/2013 Bid row (R008) adds no second contact count.\n17: SOURCE: P-005 | PAGE: 27 | REVIEW: Q1 | RELATED: same bidder later bids (R008) and signs NDA (R024)\n18: \n19: ===== R004 | Material process update | round 0 | 05/09/2013 | Mac-Gray Board of Directors =====\n20: TERMS: Board reviewed the sale-process option (risks/benefits, potential disruption, leak risk, disclosure to competitors) alongside standalone alternatives; formed a Transaction Committee (Thomas Bullock and Paul Daoust) to administer the strategic-alternatives review and recommend a financial adviser; deferred a decision to the next meeting.\n21: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n22: WHY: Stated: “the Board formed a transaction committee at this meeting, which we refer to as the Transaction Committee, and appointed Thomas Bullock and Paul Daoust, both independent directors, to serve as its members.” No sale decision or outreach yet; recorded because the governance step structures the process that follows.\n23: SOURCE: P-009; P-006 | PAGE: 28 | REVIEW: Q1 | RELATED: leads to adviser selection (R006) and sale decision (R009)\n24: \n25: ===== R005 | Adviser ended | round 0 | 05/15/2013 | BofA Merrill Lynch =====\n26: TERMS: Termination of the 2011-2012 Discussions engagement: the Transaction Committee determined on 05/14/2013 to terminate; Mac-Gray sent the termination letter 05/15/2013. Not a termination of the sale process (which had not begun).\n27: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n28: WHY: Stated: “On May 15, 2013, Mac-Gray sent a letter to BofA Merrill Lynch terminating their engagement in connection with the 2011-2012 Discussions.” Determination: “the Transaction Committee determined to terminate BofA Merrill Lynch's engagement letter entered into for purposes of the 2011-2012 Discussions.” Adviser rows carry no Count.\n29: SOURCE: P-012; P-011 | PAGE: 29 | REVIEW: Q8 | RELATED: ends the R001 mandate; new mandate in R006\n30: \n31: ===== R006 | Adviser engaged | round 0 | 05/30/2013 | BofA Merrill Lynch =====\n32: TERMS: Mac-Gray's financial advisor for the strategic-alternatives review and sale process; Board unanimously approved the engagement on 05/30/2013; engagement letter entered 05/31/2013; BofA agreed not to engage as a possible source of new acquisition financing for any potential buyer without Mac-Gray's prior written consent. Three nationally recognized banks were invited to submit proposals; the other two presented 05/29/2013.\n33: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n34: WHY: Stated: “the Board unanimously approved the engagement of BofA Merrill Lynch on the terms discussed at this meeting.” Engagement letter: “On May 31, 2013, Mac-Gray entered into an engagement letter with BofA Merrill Lynch with respect to the Board's undertaking a review of strategic alternatives, including a potential sale process, and in which BofA Merrill Lynch agreed not to engage as a possible source of new acquisition financing for any potential buyers in connection with any such sale process without Mac-Gray's prior written consent.” Adviser rows carry no Count.\n35: SOURCE: P-016; P-018 | PAGE: 29 | REVIEW: Q8 | RELATED: replaces the terminated R001 mandate\n36: \n37: ===== R007 | Activist involvement | round 0 | 05/30/2013 | Moab Partners, L.P. and affiliates =====\n38: TERMS: Proxy contest led to the election of Moab's nominees Michael M. Rothenberg and James E. Hyman at the annual meeting later on 05/30/2013 (in place of two incumbent nominees); Board reduced to seven; Moab beneficially owned approximately 9% of Mac-Gray's outstanding stock as of 06/12/2013. Mr. Rothenberg was later excluded from sale-process deliberations and engaged over a possible equity rollover (R014, R046).\n39: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n40: WHY: Stated: “Mac-Gray and Moab engaged in a proxy contest leading up to the annual meeting and Mr. Rothenberg and Mr. Hyman were elected in place of Mac-Gray's incumbent nominees” Ownership: “At the time of this meeting, Moab beneficially owned approximately 9% of Mac-Gray's outstanding common stock.” Activist rows carry no Count.\n41: SOURCE: P-017; P-019 | PAGE: 29 | REVIEW: Q1 | RELATED: context for R014 and R046 rollover rows\n42: \n43: ===== R008 | Bid | round 0 | 06/21/2013 | Party A =====\n44: TERMS: Unsolicited all-cash proposal to purchase Mac-Gray at $17.00-$19.00 per share, submitted to the Board before any confidentiality agreement. Pre-process (round 0); retained as a genuine pre-NDA offer and shown as a standing round-1 proposal (see R009). No formal documentation signal; financing not stated.\n45: FORMALITY: Informal | CONDITIONS: Heavy | COUNT: 1\n46: WHY: “On June 21, 2013, Party A submitted an unsolicited proposal to the Mac-Gray Board of Directors offering to purchase Mac-Gray for an all-cash purchase price of $17.00 to $19.00 per share” Formality: preliminary pre-NDA indication without a supported formal signal. Conditions: preliminary non-binding indication before any substantive diligence access (no data-room or management access before 08/2013), so Heavy by context; financing not addressed. Price Stated; range endpoints retained, not averaged.\n47: SOURCE: P-020 | PAGE: 30 | REVIEW: Q1; Q5; Q6 | RELATED: first contact R003; revised by R030; standing proposal counted in R1 (R009)\n48: \n49: ===== R009 | Round opened | round 1 | 06/24/2013 | Mac-Gray Board of Directors; Special Committee =====\n50: TERMS: R1 anchor: 06/24/2013 Special Committee decision to consider a sale and authorize solicitation of 50 parties — outreach followed within about a week (first reported NDA 06/28/2013); other candidate anchors rejected: R008 (06/21/2013 Party A proposal), R012 (06/28/2013 first NDA); first outreach-wave date itself not disclosed. Sale consideration concluded in stockholders' best interests; Special Committee of six independent directors established (Bullock, Daoust, Hyman, Meagher, Percelay, Robinson; Mr. Bullock chairman); Mr. MacDonald and Mr. Rothenberg excluded from sale-process evaluation and negotiations; the Transaction Committee became the Special Committee's sub-committee. Round finality: Not final (preliminary IOI solicitation; no finality signal).\n51: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n52: WHY: Stated: “the Special Committee concluded that it would be in the best interests of stockholders to consider a potential sale of Mac-Gray” Scope: “the Special Committee determined to approach a total of” “50 parties, including CSC and Pamplona, who together we refer to as CSC/Pamplona, and Party A, to solicit potential interest in a transaction with Mac-Gray, 15 of which were potential strategic buyers and 35 of which were potential financial buyers, including private equity firms.” Convention: anchor chosen under instruction rule 2 (the first outreach wave is undated; the filing reports the outreach as direct execution of the 06/24/2013 decision beginning within days). Special Committee formation and exclusions: “the Board then established the Special Committee, comprised of the six independent and disinterested members of the Board (namely, Mr. Bullock, Mr. Daoust, Mr. Hyman, William F. Meagher, Bruce A. Percelay and Alastair G. Robinson), to, among other things, explore strategic alternatives to enhance value to Mac-Gray's stockholders and to recommend to the Board the advisability of any such strategic alternative. In connection with this delegation of authority, the Special Committee was further authorized to (a) consider and evaluate all proposals that might be received by Mac-Gray in connection with a possible sale or other significant business transaction, through any form of transaction, including, without limitation, merger, stock purchase, asset purchase, recapitalization, reorganization, going-private transaction, consolidation, amalgamation or other transaction, (b) participate in and direct the negotiation of the material terms and conditions of any such transaction, (c) consider any alternatives to any such transaction, including without limitation, Mac-Gray's continuing to operate as an independent company and (d) recommend to the Board the advisability of entering into a definitive agreement with respect to any such transaction. Mr. Bullock was ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n53: SOURCE: P-021; P-022; P-023; P-024; P-025 | PAGE: 30 | REVIEW: Q1 | RELATED: candidate anchors R008, R012; deadline set R010\n54: \n55: ===== R010 | Deadline set | round 1 | by 07/23/2013; communication day not disclosed (packages furnished from 06/28/2013) | Mac-Gray / BofA Merrill Lynch =====\n56: TERMS: Preliminary indications of interest due 07/23/2013; parties signing confidentiality and standstill agreements were furnished an informational package and instructed to submit by that date. The letter asked proposals to address valuation, sources and structures of financing, material conditions (including required approvals) and due-diligence focus areas.\n57: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n58: WHY: Stated: “Parties that had entered into confidentiality and standstill agreements with Mac-Gray were furnished an informational package for the purpose of providing potential bidders with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest, which they were instructed to submit by July 23, 2013.” Date: the communication day is not disclosed; Working date assigned by sequence (this row follows the 06/24/2013 round opening, R009). Due date is the only bid-submission clock recorded here.\n59: SOURCE: P-026 | PAGE: 32 | REVIEW: Q2; Q8 | RELATED: milestone R017\n60: \n61: ===== R011 | Contact | round 1 | 06/28/2013 | CSC/Pamplona =====\n62: TERMS: Direction: target-initiated (BofA Merrill Lynch spoke with Pamplona as instructed by the Special Committee). Pamplona expressed interest in a transaction, including an acquisition by portfolio company CSC; said business and legal due diligence would be minimal given familiarity with Mac-Gray and the industry. First reported contact with the winning bidder unit.\n63: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: 1\n64: WHY: Stated: “BofA Merrill Lynch also reported that they had spoken with representatives of Pamplona, as instructed by the Special Committee, who expressed interest in a transaction, including an acquisition of Mac-Gray by its portfolio company, CSC.” “Pamplona emphasized that business and legal due diligence would be minimal given its familiarity with Mac-Gray and the industry generally.” Count 1 = the newly represented bidder unit for the contact step.\n65: SOURCE: P-027 | PAGE: 32 | REVIEW: Q1 | RELATED: NDA R015; bidding through R040; winner\n66: \n67: ===== R012 | NDA signed | round 1 | 06/28/2013 | Party B =====\n68: TERMS: Confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray (Mac-Gray form; terms individually negotiated but not materially varied); furnished the informational package to formulate a preliminary indication of interest. New signer; NDA count 1.\n69: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: 1\n70: WHY: Stated: “Later on June 28, 2013, Party B, entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party B with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.” Form and standstill terms: “entered into confidentiality agreements with Mac-Gray on a form provided by Mac-Gray, the terms of which were individually negotiated with potential bidders, but did not vary materially from the form provided.” Count 1 = newly represented signer.\n71: SOURCE: P-028; P-026 | PAGE: 32 | REVIEW: Q3 | RELATED: bid R020, R029, R037\n72: \n73: ===== R013 | NDA signed | round 1 | 06/30/2013 | Party C =====\n74: TERMS: Confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray; furnished the informational package to formulate a preliminary indication of interest. New signer; NDA count 1.\n75: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: 1\n76: WHY: Stated: “On June 30, 2013, Party C entered into a confidentiality and standstill agreement with Mac-Gray and was provided with an informational package for the purpose of providing Party C with data to formulate a preliminary indication of interest.” Count 1 = newly represented signer.\n77: SOURCE: P-029; P-026 | PAGE: 32 | REVIEW: Q3 | RELATED: bids R021, R023, R031; exit R038\n78: \n79: ===== R014 | Material process update | round 1 | 07/06/2013 | Moab (with Mac-Gray Special Committee) =====\n80: TERMS: Rollover: Special Committee denied, at this stage, Moab's request to partner exclusively with CSC/Pamplona and to discuss an equity rollover; instructed BofA Merrill Lynch and Goodwin Procter to communicate that rollover discussions were premature but would be permitted at a later stage, so Moab's shares could remain available as a capital source for other bidders. Denial step of this rollover relationship (resolution in R046).\n81: FORMALITY:  | CONDITIONS:  | COUNT: \n82: WHY: Stated: “The Special Committee discussed whether Moab should be permitted to partner exclusively with CSC/Pamplona.” Decision: “the Special Committee decided to deny